\statement{A.80}{Proved}{An odd relation-module lift and a finite negative action with no full involutory realization, without prescribing a cusp normalization}
Let \(\Pi=C_2\amalg C_3=\langle s,r\rangle\),
\(\tau=sr\), and let \(q:\Pi\twoheadrightarrow Q\) be a finite
quotient injecting the two factors. Write \(s_0=q(s)\),
\(r_0=q(r)\), and \(t=q(\tau)\). Suppose that
\(b_0\in\operatorname{Aut}(Q)\) and \(\beta_0,J_0\in Q\) satisfy
\[
 \begin{gathered}
 b_0^2=1,\qquad b_0(t)=t^{-1},\\
 b_0(s_0)=\beta_0s_0\beta_0^{-1},\qquad
 b_0(r_0)=J_0r_0^{-1}J_0^{-1},\\
 b_0(\beta_0)\beta_0=b_0(J_0)J_0=1.
 \end{gathered}
 \tag{A.80.1}
\]
For every prime \(p\nmid2|Q|\), put
\[
 L=\ker q,\qquad
 \Phi_p(L)=\overline{[L,L]L^p},\qquad
 G_p=\Pi/\Phi_p(L).
 \tag{A.80.2}
\]
There are an involution \(b_p\in\operatorname{Aut}(G_p)\)
above \(b_0\) and lifts \(\beta_p,J_p\) of the prescribed
\(\beta_0,J_0\) such that
\[
 \begin{gathered}
 b_p(\tau)=\tau^{-1},\qquad
 b_p(s)=\beta_ps\beta_p^{-1},\qquad
 b_p(r)=J_pr^{-1}J_p^{-1},\\
 b_p(\beta_p)\beta_p=b_p(J_p)J_p=1.
 \end{gathered}
 \tag{A.80.3}
\]
These are actual first relation-module quotients, of order
\[
                      |G_p|=|Q|p^{1+|Q|/6}.
 \tag{A.80.4}
\]

Apply this assertion to the specified \(Q,h_0\) of A.79,
\(b_0=h_0c_Q\), and \(p=5\). The resulting finite negative
involution \(b_5\) is not induced by any full negative peripheral
involution of \(\Pi\), even if the full cusp is only required to
be inverted up to conjugacy. Every permitted semisimple extension
chosen in the proof gives such a finite action. Equivalently, the finite positive
peripheral action \(h_5=b_5c_{G_5}\) has no full positive
peripheral lift whose negative companion has order two. Its
order-two realization fails without prescribing any full cusp
normalization or any full normalizer frame.

The existence of an ordinary full positive lift of this new
\(h_5\), or of compatible actions on its actual central
refinements, is not asserted. Thus this conclusion does not
extend A.79's central-refinement assertion to a newly chosen
coefficient action. It also does not exclude the surviving
\(W=0\) actions or settle the main existential question.

\begin{proof}
\emph{An index-two semisimple extension lemma.}\par
We first prove the precise positivity fact needed below. Let
\(Q\lhd E\) have index two, let k be a field whose
characteristic does not divide \(|E|\), and let X be an actual
finite-dimensional kQ-module. If its class in \(K_0(kQ)\)
belongs to the restriction image of \(K_0(kE)\), then X is
the restriction of an actual kE-module. Here \(K_0\) is the
free abelian group on the simple modules. Semisimplicity over
the stated field follows from the general-field form of
Maschke's theorem; see \citet[Theorem~7.4 and
Proposition~7.9]{AMilne2025}.

For completeness, the induction adjunction used in the proof
is the explicit bijection
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \operatorname{Hom}_{kE}(kE\otimes_{kQ}S,T)
       &\longrightarrow\operatorname{Hom}_{kQ}(S,T),\\
 F&\longmapsto\bigl(u\mapsto F(1\otimes u)\bigr).
 \end{aligned}
 \tag{A.80.5}
\]
Its inverse sends f to \(g\otimes u\mapsto g f(u)\).
The tensor relation makes the inverse well defined, so this
is valid over k without any complex-character assumption.

Fix a simple kQ-module S and let \(S^b\) denote its twist
by an element of \(E\setminus Q\). If \(S^b\not\simeq S\),
then
\[
 \operatorname{Res}_Q^E\operatorname{Ind}_Q^E S
           \simeq S\oplus S^b.
\]
Every E-endomorphism preserves the S-summand, and its action
there determines its action on the other summand. Thus its
endomorphism ring is \(\operatorname{End}_{kQ}(S)\), a
division ring. Semisimplicity makes \(\operatorname{Ind}S\)
simple. By (A.80.5), every E-simple whose restriction contains
S is this one. On this two-element orbit the restriction
semigroup therefore contains exactly the nonnegative multiples
of \([S]+[S^b]\), which are precisely the positive classes in
its restriction lattice.

If \(S^b\simeq S\), decompose
\[
 \operatorname{Ind}_Q^E S\simeq\bigoplus_i T_i^{n_i},
 \qquad \operatorname{Res}_Q^E T_i\simeq S^{e_i}.
\]
Restriction gives \(\sum_i n_i e_i=2\). Every E-simple
supported on S occurs among these \(T_i\), by (A.80.5).
Consequently all available restriction multiplicities are one,
or the sole available multiplicity is two. Every nonnegative
integer in their restriction lattice is again realized by an
actual nonnegative sum of E-simples. The Q-isotypic support of
an E-simple is one orbit under \(E/Q\), since an orbit sum is
E-stable. Applying the preceding two cases independently to
these disjoint orbits proves the lemma. This is an index-two
argument, not a general positivity assertion for restriction.

\emph{The actual relation module has a virtual extension.}\par
Set \(k=\mathbb F_p\), \(M=L/\Phi_p(L)\), and
\(E=Q\rtimes_{b_0}\langle\epsilon\rangle\), where
\(\epsilon^2=1\). The two elements
\[
                 d_s=\beta_0^{-1}\epsilon,
                 \qquad d_r=J_0^{-1}\epsilon
 \tag{A.80.6}
\]
are involutions. The subgroups
\(A_s=\langle s_0,d_s\rangle\simeq C_2\times C_2\)
and \(A_r=\langle r_0,d_r\rangle\simeq S_3\) have
intersections \(\langle s_0\rangle\) and
\(\langle r_0\rangle\) with Q, respectively. This is
where both actual norm-one frame hypotheses are used.

The Bass--Serre cover of the source has edge set Q and vertex
sets \(Q/\langle s_0\rangle\) and
\(Q/\langle r_0\rangle\). Its first homology is exactly M,
so its augmented cellular sequence gives
\[
 \begin{split}
 0\longrightarrow M\longrightarrow k[Q]\longrightarrow
 k[Q/\langle s_0\rangle]\oplus k[Q/\langle r_0\rangle]
 \longrightarrow k\longrightarrow0.
 \end{split}
 \tag{A.80.7}
\]
One can equally form this finite graph for the discrete modular
group and complete its finite-index free subgroup; the induced
topology on an open subgroup is full. Thus (A.80.7) computes
the actual source relation module. Its dimension is
\(1+|Q|/6\), proving (A.80.4), as in A.77.

Each term on the right of the corresponding virtual identity
\[
 [M]=[k]+[k[Q]]-[k[Q/\langle s_0\rangle]]
                   -[k[Q/\langle r_0\rangle]]
 \tag{A.80.8}
\]
is the restriction of an E-module. Use the trivial module,
\(k[E/\langle\epsilon\rangle]\), \(k[E/A_s]\), and
\(k[E/A_r]\), respectively. It follows that \([M]\) lies
in the restriction image. We are not asserting that the
particular differential in (A.80.7) is E-equivariant.

\emph{Extend the actual cusp submodule with negative sign.}\par
Put \(m=\operatorname{ord}(t)\). The torsion-free discrete
cover underlying L is an oriented surface with
\([Q:\langle t\rangle]\) cusps, all of width m. The
positive boundary classes have only their total-sum relation.
Hence their Q-submodule P in M is
\[
 P\simeq
 k[Q/\langle t\rangle]\big/
 k\!\left(\sum_{g\langle t\rangle}e_{g\langle t\rangle}\right),
 \qquad e_{\langle t\rangle}\longmapsto[\tau^m].
 \tag{A.80.9}
\]
This is the same elementary boundary-homology calculation used
in C.29. When there is one cusp the quotient is zero.
Because \(b_0(t)=t^{-1}\), the rule
\[
       \epsilon e_{g\langle t\rangle}
              =-e_{b_0(g)\langle t\rangle}
 \tag{A.80.10}
\]
gives an E-module extending this Q-module and preserves its
sole relation. It sends \([\tau^m]\) to its negative.

Maschke supplies a Q-splitting \(M=P\oplus X\).
Since \([X]=[M]-[P]\) is in the restriction image, the
index-two lemma extends X to an actual E-module. Combine
that extension with (A.80.10). On the actual M this gives
an invertible k-linear map B satisfying
\[
 B(qv)=b_0(q)B(v),\qquad B^2=1,
 \qquad B[\tau^m]=-[\tau^m].
 \tag{A.80.11}
\]
The action on P has been retained literally under the chosen
Q-splitting; it is not merely an equality of virtual classes.

\emph{The cusp equation and the odd norm on the actual group.}\par
The finite extension \(1\to M\to G_p\to Q\to1\)
splits because \(p\nmid|Q|\). This follows, for example,
by coprime averaging in \(H^2(Q,M)\). Choose a splitting
and write \(G_p=M\rtimes Q\), with the actual source
cusp \(\tau=(a,t)\). Set
\[
        \bar a=\frac1m\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}t^j a.
        \qquad \tau^m=m\bar a\in M.
 \tag{A.80.12}
\]
Equation (A.80.11) says \(B\bar a=-\bar a\).
Therefore the right side of
\[
                   (1-t^{-1})k=-t^{-1}a-Ba
 \tag{A.80.13}
\]
has zero t-average. Coprime averaging identifies that kernel
with \((1-t^{-1})M\), so choose a solution k. The map
\[
 b_*(v,q)=\bigl(Bv+(1-b_0(q))k,\ b_0(q)\bigr)
 \tag{A.80.14}
\]
is an automorphism inducing \(b_0\), and (A.80.13) is
exactly \(b_*(\tau)=\tau^{-1}\). Its square is
\(\operatorname{Inn}(v_0)\), where \(v_0=k+Bk\).
The equality \(b_*^2(\tau)=\tau\) gives
\(v_0\in M^t\), and \(Bv_0=v_0\). Thus
\[
                        b_p=\operatorname{Inn}(-v_0/2)b_*
 \tag{A.80.15}
\]
still inverts the literal cusp and has square one. This
correction is allowed because p is odd and lies in the actual
cusp centralizer. No odd-order assertion about the entire
automorphism kernel has been used.

\emph{Retain both prescribed lower frame values.}\par
Work in \(\widetilde E=G_p\rtimes_{b_p}C_2\), which
maps to E. For \(A=\langle s\rangle\) or
\(\langle r\rangle\), the normalizer maps onto the
normalizer of \(A_Q\). Indeed a lift of an element of
the latter conjugates A to another complement in the
preimage of \(A_Q\). Coprime complement conjugacy moves
it back to A by an element of M. In this elementary-abelian
case this is just \(H^1(A_Q,M)=0\), proved by averaging.
The kernel of the normalizer map is \(C_M(A)\): a
normalizing element of M acts trivially on A because
\(A\to A_Q\) is injective.

Choose a normalizer lift z of the prescribed \(d_s\) or
\(d_r\) in (A.80.6). Its square is a vector w in
\(C_M(A)\), fixed by conjugation by z. Replacing z by
\((-w/2)z\) makes its square one without changing its
image or its normalizing property. Its action on A is the
required one, since that action is already prescribed on
the faithfully mapped \(A_Q\). Writing the two adjusted
elements as \(\beta_p^{-1}\epsilon\) and
\(J_p^{-1}\epsilon\) proves all of (A.80.3), with
the literal prescribed lower values. These are two separate
normalizer choices; no additional common transporter is
asserted. The resulting \(b_p\) is nontrivial: if Q is
cyclic it is C6 and cusp inversion is already nontrivial
there; otherwise (A.80.9) makes the actual cusp have
nontrivial p-part, which is inverted.

\emph{The odd relation quotient detects every cusp-conjugator head.}\par
For any noncyclic factor-faithful Q and any odd p, not
necessarily prime to \(|Q|\), the same boundary calculation
gives
\[
          \operatorname{im}\bigl(C_{G_p}(\tau)\to Q\bigr)
                        =\langle t\rangle.
 \tag{A.80.16}
\]
There are \(r=|Q|/m\geq2\) positive boundary classes.
They are distinct over \(\mathbb F_p\): if \(r\geq3\),
the difference of two coordinate vectors is not a multiple
of the total-sum relation; if \(r=2\), the two nonzero
classes are opposite and p is odd. A centralizer of
\(\tau\) fixes \([\tau^m]\), so its deck action fixes
the identity cusp coset and its Q-image lies in
\(\langle t\rangle\). Powers of the actual cusp give
the reverse inclusion.

In particular,
\[
                   C_\Pi(\tau)=\overline{\langle\tau\rangle}
                              \simeq\widehat{\mathbb Z}.
 \tag{A.80.17}
\]
For the centralizer equality, refine every finite quotient
by the standard S3 quotient and apply (A.80.16);
compactness of the closed procyclic subgroup gives the
claim. Its full \(\widehat{\mathbb Z}\)-type can be
checked without a torsion assumption: modulo every integer
n the matrices
\[
 \begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
 \begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&1\end{pmatrix}
\]
define a source quotient in
\(\operatorname{SL}_2(\mathbb Z/n)/\{\pm I\}\), and
their product is the class of
\(\left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&1\\0&1\end{smallmatrix}\right)\),
of exact order n.

\emph{Involution-preserving normalization and the A.79 consequence.}\par
Suppose that a full negative involution b induces the
constructed \(b_5\), and write
\[
                        b(\tau)=u\tau^{-1}u^{-1}.
\]
Since \(b^2=1\), the element \(z=b(u)u\) centralizes
\(\tau\). On the one hand \(b(z)=u z u^{-1}\);
on the other hand (A.80.17) and cusp inversion give
\(b(z)=u z^{-1}u^{-1}\). Hence \(z^2=1\), and
(A.80.17) forces \(z=1\).

The image of u in \(G_5\) centralizes the actual cusp,
because \(b_5\) inverts it literally. By (A.80.16),
\(u_Q\in\langle t\rangle\). Choose
\(v\in\widehat{\mathbb Z}\) with \(u_Q=t^v\), and
replace u by \(u\tau^{-v}\). This changes neither its
cusp-conjugacy equation nor its norm-one property, and now
\(u_Q=1\). The automorphism
\[
                           b'=\operatorname{Inn}(u^{-1})b
 \tag{A.80.18}
\]
remains an involution, inverts \(\tau\) literally, and
still induces \(b_0\) on Q. Then \(h=b'c\) is a full
positive peripheral automorphism fixing \(\tau\) literally
and inducing \(h_0\). This contradicts A.79.

All hypotheses for the construction hold for that Q: its
order is \(2^{972}\,72\,7^{13}\), its retained quotient
frames satisfy (A.80.1), and \(5\nmid|Q|\). Canonical c
preserves \(L\) and its characteristic subgroup
\(\Phi_5(L)\), so \(c_{G_5}\) and \(h_5=b_5c_{G_5}\)
are actual automorphisms of the same finite source quotient.
This proves the stated finite nonrealization assertion.
The semisimple choice in (A.80.11) does not supply an
ordinary full realization or a lift to a further source-central
quotient, and none has been used.
\end{proof}
